  "worked_solution": "Since $2,$ $5,$ and $7$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $2\\cdot 5\\cdot 7=70.$\n\nWe will solve the system in two steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the common solution for the first and second congruences of the system.\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,⟹\\,x=1+2t,\\,t\\in Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡3 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 1+2t & ≡3 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 2t & ≡2 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ t & ≡1 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $t=1+5s,$ where $s\\in Z.$ Substituting this into the expression for $x,$ we get the common solution for the first and second congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1+2t \\\\  & =1+2(1+5s) \\\\  & =3+10s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Find the common solution of all three congruences.\n\nNow, substituting the common solution of the first two congruences into the third congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡5 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ 3+10s & ≡5 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ 10s & ≡2 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ 3s & ≡2 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $7$ is a relatively small modulus, by trial and error, we have that $5\\cdot 3≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$ So, we multiply both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3s & ≡2 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3s & ≡10 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ s & ≡3 &  & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $s=3+7u,$ where $u\\in Z.$ Substituting this into the expression for the common solution of the first two congruences, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3+10s \\\\  & =3+10(3+7u) \\\\  & =33+70u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x≡33\\,(\\text{mod}\\,70)$ is the unique solution modulo $70,$ and $x=33$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system.",
